Braille Support for the GRUB Boot Loader
==========================================

BRLTTY can run as a loadable GRUB module, providing braille display
accessibility during the boot menu. This allows blind users to read
and navigate the GRUB menu, interact with the GRUB command line, and
edit boot entries using a refreshable braille display.

The module integrates with GRUB's terminal system: screen content is
captured via a shadow terminal buffer, and braille key presses are
translated into GRUB keystrokes injected back into the input queue.
Polling happens cooperatively through GRUB's input loop — each time
GRUB checks for keyboard input, BRLTTY gets a chance to update the
braille display and process key events.

Supported Braille Displays
--------------------------

All USB braille displays supported by BRLTTY should work.

GRUB's USB stack has drivers for UHCI, OHCI, and EHCI controllers
(USB 1.x and 2.0). It does not have a native xHCI (USB 3.0) driver.
However, on most UEFI systems this does not matter: the firmware
provides USB Legacy Support, which emulates USB 2.0 behavior for
devices on xHCI-controlled ports. GRUB's EHCI driver sees the device
through this emulation layer without knowing xHCI is involved.

This means braille displays should work on any USB port of a typical
UEFI system, as long as "USB Legacy Support" is enabled in the BIOS
settings (it usually is by default).

Systems where USB may not work:

* Coreboot-based systems without runtime firmware emulation — GRUB
  gets no USB on xHCI-only platforms without a native xHCI driver.
* Some newer Intel boards (post-Skylake) that dropped EHCI entirely
  and have weak or absent USB legacy emulation in their UEFI.
* Headless/embedded boards without BIOS USB legacy support.

Serial-attached braille displays are also supported via GRUB's serial
port driver.

Bluetooth braille displays are not supported — GRUB has no Bluetooth
stack.

Speech output is not available — GRUB has no audio subsystem.

Prerequisites
-------------

Only a standard GCC toolchain is needed — no cross-compiler is required.
The native system GCC is used with ``-m32`` or ``-m64`` as appropriate
for the target GRUB platform.

Fedora/RHEL::

      dnf install gcc make autoconf

Debian/Ubuntu::

      apt install gcc make autoconf

Building
--------

The module must be built against a configured GRUB source tree. There is no
``grub-devel`` package providing the required headers.

1. Obtain the GRUB source::

      git clone https://git.savannah.gnu.org/git/grub.git /path/to/grub

   Alternatively, use your distribution's GRUB source::

      dnf download --source grub2        # Fedora/RHEL
      apt-get source grub2               # Debian/Ubuntu

2. Bootstrap and configure the GRUB source tree::

      cd /path/to/grub
      ./bootstrap
      ./configure --target=x86_64 --with-platform=efi

   For Legacy BIOS builds::

      ./configure --target=i386 --with-platform=pc

   A full GRUB ``make`` is not required — only ``./configure`` is needed
   to generate ``config.h`` and the header symlinks (``grub/cpu``,
   ``grub/machine``) that BRLTTY's build depends on.

3. Create a symlink named ``grub-root`` in the BRLTTY source root pointing to
   the GRUB source tree::

      cd /path/to/brltty
      ln -s /path/to/grub grub-root

4. Configure the BRLTTY build::

      ./cfg-grub --with-grub-platform=x86_64-efi

   For Legacy BIOS builds::

      ./cfg-grub --with-grub-platform=i386-pc

   If the target system has GRUB installed, the platform can be auto-detected
   by omitting the ``--with-grub-platform`` option. Distro packagers building
   for multiple platforms should always specify it explicitly.

   Additional configure options may be passed through::

      ./cfg-grub --with-grub-platform=x86_64-efi --with-braille-driver=hw

5. Build the GRUB module::

      make

   When configured for a GRUB platform, ``make`` automatically builds
   ``brltty.mod`` instead of the normal host targets.

Building for Multiple Platforms
-------------------------------

Distros typically ship GRUB for both Legacy BIOS and UEFI. Build the module
separately for each platform:

1. Configure and build for UEFI::

      cd /path/to/grub
      ./configure --target=x86_64 --with-platform=efi
      cd /path/to/brltty
      ./cfg-grub --with-grub-platform=x86_64-efi
      make
      cp Programs/brltty.mod /output/x86_64-efi/

2. Reconfigure and build for Legacy BIOS::

      cd /path/to/grub
      ./configure --target=i386 --with-platform=pc
      cd /path/to/brltty
      make clean
      ./cfg-grub --with-grub-platform=i386-pc
      make
      cp Programs/brltty.mod /output/i386-pc/

Installation
------------

1. Copy ``brltty.mod`` to the GRUB modules directory::

      # EFI systems:
      cp brltty.mod /boot/grub/x86_64-efi/

      # Legacy BIOS systems:
      cp brltty.mod /boot/grub/i386-pc/

2. Install key tables and text tables. BRLTTY loads these from disk at
   runtime — without them, braille key bindings will not work::

      mkdir -p /boot/grub/brltty
      cp -r Tables/Input /boot/grub/brltty/
      cp -r Tables/Text  /boot/grub/brltty/

   The tables directory must match the ``-T`` argument passed to BRLTTY
   (default: ``/boot/grub/brltty``).

3. To load the module automatically at every boot, add the following to
   ``/etc/default/grub``::

      GRUB_PRELOAD_MODULES="usb uhci ohci ehci brltty"

   Then regenerate the GRUB configuration::

      grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg

   On some distributions this is done with::

      update-grub

   Loading all three USB host controller modules (``uhci``, ``ohci``,
   ``ehci``) is harmless and ensures the display works regardless of the
   actual USB controller. On UEFI systems with USB Legacy Support, the
   ``ehci`` module is typically sufficient even for xHCI ports.

Configuration
-------------

BRLTTY's options can be configured through GRUB environment variables set
in ``grub.cfg``. The module reads these via GRUB's environment API::

   # In grub.cfg (before insmod brltty):
   set BRLTTY_BRAILLE_DRIVER=hw
   set BRLTTY_TEXT_TABLE=en_US

The module currently starts BRLTTY with a hardcoded set of arguments
equivalent to::

   brltty -l debug -n -T /boot/grub/brltty

The ``-T`` flag sets the tables directory. The ``-n`` flag disables
daemonization (not meaningful in GRUB, but required by the option parser).

Loading Manually
----------------

From the GRUB command line, load the USB stack and then the BRLTTY module::

   grub> insmod usb
   grub> insmod uhci
   grub> insmod brltty

Replace ``uhci`` with ``ohci`` or ``ehci`` as appropriate for your USB
controller. You can load multiple controller modules. The braille display
must be plugged in before loading the brltty module.

For serial-attached displays, load the serial module instead::

   grub> insmod serial
   grub> insmod brltty

How It Works
------------

The module integrates with GRUB through two mechanisms:

**Screen capture**: BRLTTY registers a wrapper ``grub_term_output`` terminal
that interposes on the active display terminal. All output (character writes,
cursor movement, screen clears) is captured into a shadow buffer while being
forwarded to the real terminal. BRLTTY's screen driver reads from this shadow
buffer to render content on the braille display.

**Key injection**: BRLTTY registers a ``grub_term_input`` terminal whose
``getkey()`` callback calls ``brlttyWait(0)`` — one non-blocking pass through
the event loop. This fires timer callbacks, polls USB for braille key events,
and refreshes the display. Braille key presses are translated to GRUB
keystrokes and returned through the input terminal.

This cooperative polling model means BRLTTY runs whenever GRUB checks for
input — during menu display, command line editing, or any interactive prompt.

Testing with QEMU
------------------

The repository includes test scripts for running the module in QEMU with
USB passthrough of a real braille display:

1. Build the module (see Building above).

2. Build the test disk image::

      ./grub-test build-image

3. Run the test. The ``go-grub`` script auto-detects the braille display
   used by the host BRLTTY, stops host BRLTTY, runs QEMU with USB
   passthrough, and restarts host BRLTTY afterwards::

      sudo ./go-grub

   Output is logged to ``grub-test.d/go.log`` for review after the test
   (since the braille display is unavailable during the QEMU run).

   The ``grub-test`` script can also be run directly::

      ./grub-test run --usb-host=VENDOR:PRODUCT

   Use ``./grub-test --help`` for all options including ``--nographic``
   for an interactive GRUB serial console.

Prerequisites for QEMU testing::

   # Fedora:
   dnf install qemu-system-x86-core edk2-ovmf mtools \
               grub2-efi-x64-modules qemu-device-usb-host socat

Troubleshooting
---------------

Display not detected
   Ensure the USB modules are loaded before ``brltty.mod``. The display must
   be plugged in before loading the module.

Keys not responding
   Ensure key table files are installed under ``/boot/grub/brltty/Input/``.
   Without key tables, BRLTTY receives key events but cannot map them to
   commands. Check that the table directory matches the ``-T`` argument.

Only some USB ports work
   Verify that "USB Legacy Support" is enabled in the BIOS settings —
   this allows the firmware to emulate USB 2.0 for xHCI ports. If some
   ports still do not work, try different ports — on some systems, only
   specific ports have legacy emulation. As a last resort, a USB 2.0
   hub or add-in card with a UHCI/OHCI/EHCI controller will bypass the
   firmware emulation entirely.

Display shows only cursor, no text
   The braille display shows screen content captured from GRUB's terminal
   output. If GRUB has not produced any output yet (e.g., waiting at a
   blank menu), the display may appear empty except for the cursor.

Module fails to load
   The ``brltty.mod`` file must match the GRUB platform (x86_64-efi vs
   i386-pc) and should be built against a GRUB source tree close to the
   installed version.

Limitations
-----------

* Bluetooth is not supported.
* Speech is not supported.
* GRUB has no native xHCI (USB 3.0) driver. On UEFI systems this is
  transparent thanks to firmware USB Legacy Support emulation. On
  coreboot or systems without USB legacy emulation, USB braille
  displays will not work unless a UHCI/OHCI/EHCI controller is available.
* GRUB's ``grub_snprintf`` does not support the ``%.*s`` format specifier
  (precision via ``*`` argument). BRLTTY code that uses this has been
  patched to use direct memory operations instead.
* GRUB's ``grub_file_open`` does not handle ``..`` in file paths. The
  ``fopen`` shim normalizes paths before opening.
* The module must be compiled from source against a configured GRUB source
  tree. The GRUB version should match (or be close to) the installed version.
* File access is read-only — preferences cannot be saved.
